= Identity =

== Overview ==
'''Identity''' is strict sameness: the condition under which something is one and the same thing.

In formal notation:

<pre>
x = y
</pre>

states that x and y are identical.

Identity should be distinguished from similarity, equivalence and having the same properties.

== Numerical and qualitative identity ==
'''Numerical identity''' means being one and the same entity.

'''Qualitative identity''' means sharing relevant properties.

Two mass-produced bolts can be qualitatively indistinguishable while remaining two numerically distinct bolts.

Conversely, one object can change many qualities over time while still being treated as numerically the same object.

== Identity and indiscernibility ==
Leibniz's law is often expressed as the principle that identical things share all properties:

<pre>
x = y → for every property P, P(x) ↔ P(y)
</pre>

The converse—whether sharing every property guarantees identity—is connected to the principle of the identity of indiscernibles and is philosophically contested depending on how properties are understood.

== Identity over time ==
Persistence creates difficult identity questions.

When is a later entity the same entity as an earlier one?

Possible criteria include:
* material continuity;
* structural continuity;
* causal continuity;
* biological continuity;
* legal continuity;
* functional continuity.

Different types of entity can use different criteria.

A corporation, organism and software project need not persist in the same way.

== Identity and identifiers ==
An '''identifier''' is a symbol or token used to refer to something.

Identity is the relation of sameness itself.

One entity can have multiple identifiers, and one poorly governed identifier can accidentally be reused for several entities.

Therefore:

<pre>
same identifier ≠ guaranteed same entity
different identifier ≠ guaranteed different entity
</pre>

Identity systems require rules connecting identifiers to referents.

== Identity and reference ==
Two names can refer to the same entity.

For example, "Hesperus" and "Phosphorus" were historically used for what is now known to be the same planet, Venus.

The identity of the referent does not imply that the names have identical meaning or that every speaker knows the identity.

This is a classical problem linking identity and [[Semantics|semantics]].

== Identity in knowledge representation ==
[[Knowledge representation]] systems need identity to merge descriptions and prevent duplicate entities.

Identity statements can support:
* data integration;
* graph merging;
* provenance linkage;
* entity resolution.

But incorrect identity merges can propagate many false inferences.

Identity assertions should therefore be stronger than loose similarity or matching.

== OWL sameAs ==
In OWL:

<pre>
a owl:sameAs b
</pre>

asserts that a and b denote the same individual.

This is a strong logical identity relation.

It should not be used merely because:
* labels match;
* records seem similar;
* two pages discuss related subjects.

Approximate correspondence should use weaker mapping relations.

== Identity versus equivalence ==
Two objects can be equivalent for a purpose without being identical.

Examples:
* two files with equivalent content;
* two mathematical expressions with equal value;
* two implementations with equivalent observable behavior;
* two concepts mapped as close matches.

Equivalence relations specify sameness under selected criteria; identity claims strict sameness.

== Personal and social identity ==
"Identity" also appears in psychology and social science to mean self-conception, social role or group membership.

Those are legitimate specialized uses but are different from the strict metaphysical/logical notion discussed here.

== Epistemic notes ==
Identity statements are often highly consequential in data systems because properties associated with one identifier may be transferred to the other. A strong identity relation should therefore be asserted only when its semantics are intended.

